{"answer":"The critic's case concedes the target's own boundedness premise but then misdescribes the construction's semantics and overstates what the critic establishes.\n\n(1) 'Validity of every committed root' clause. The critic claims an originally committed, unattacked root A_i is 'no longer attack-free' because it must attack C, and C's acceptance becomes a precondition for defeating B. But under standard bipolar/accrual encodings, accepted arguments do not 'attack' the collector; attackers of each A_i are redirected to C, not A_i itself. If A_i is an unattacked accepted root, it has no attackers to redirect, so nothing about A_i changes. The critic has not established that a root A_i acquires an attack obligation or loses direct validity; that is an inference the critic draws from a nonstandard reading ('must now attack C'), not a fact fixed by the target. The target states C is accepted exactly when every A_i is accepted; that is a semantic constraint on C's status, not a claim that root validity is mediated through C. The critic's alternative characterization may show a risk under a different encoding, but it has not been shown for the target as specified.\n\n(2) 'Determinism of the fixed point' clause. The critic argues overlapping accrual sets can produce cyclic/overlapping dependencies whose unique grounded extension is not guaranteed without a boundedness condition. But uniqueness of the grounded extension is a general property of Dung-style grounded semantics for finite frameworks; it is not contingent on the boundedness of accrual sets. What boundedness secures is the polynomial/linear complexity and absence of blow-up, which the target explicitly assumes ('for a bounded set'). The target already flags that overlapping accrual sets can blow up unless bounded. Thus the unbounded-overlap concern is a known limitation, not an omitted condition affecting determinism. The critic has not actually established non-uniqueness or indeterminism of the grounded extension for the finite, bounded construction; the claim that uniqueness is 'not guaranteed' is asserted, not proven.\n\n(3) The critic also does not engage the target's acknowledged limitations in a way that refutes it: the target concedes assumptions about independent attacks and possible unintended defense paths through C. These are genuine points of criticism, but they are already admitted by the target, so they do not establish a defect beyond what the construction itself acknowledges.\n\nIn sum: the root-validity objection relies on a mischaracterization of which nodes are redirected and what C's acceptance condition means; the determinism objection conflates complexity/avoidance-of-blow-up with uniqueness of the grounded extension, which is not shown to fail. The critic has not established the claimed violation of the committed-root guarantee or the claimed indeterminism of the fixed point for the bounded construction as specified."}